<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Balearica</h1><p class="st-meta">369 CE – 455 CE (approx.) · capital Palma</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The late Roman province of the Balearic Islands, separated from Carthaginiensis in the 4th century and lost to the Vandals in 455.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a>, conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 123 BCE, belonged to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Tarraconensis</a> and then <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/carthaginiensis">Carthaginiensis</a>. Around 369 they became a separate province, Balearica, under a governor in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/diocese-of-hispania">Diocese of Hispania</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The islands lived from farming, fishing and trade, with towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pollentia">Pollentia</a> and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahon">Mago</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciutadella-de-menorca">Iamo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> was well established by the 5th century. A letter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severus-of-minorca">Severus of Minorca</a> describes the forced conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Mago in 418, a rare window on island life.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Vandal raids from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> struck the islands from 425. Around 455 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> under Gaiseric took control, ending Roman rule. The islands passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>' reconquest of Africa in 534.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 369 CE – 455 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:67.04%;width:1.71%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>369 CE – 455 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Palma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Insulae Balearum</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=412&amp;focus=balearica" aria-label="Balearica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4745 761 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Balearica lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5065 1017L5304 942M5065 1017L4975 1081M5065 1017L5259 1106"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M4975 1081h0M5259 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5065 1017h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5065 1017h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Palma. <a href="/atlas?year=412&amp;focus=balearica">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
